---
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Bars from '../../components/Bars.astro';
import specNormal from '../../assets/andon/spec-normal.webp';
import specFault from '../../assets/andon/spec-fault.webp';
import AucChart from '../../components/AucChart.astro';
import AndonReplay from '../../components/AndonReplay.astro';
import ProjectTrail from '../../components/ProjectTrail.astro';
import { andon } from '../../data/metrics';
import { projects } from '../../data/projects';
import '../../styles/project.css';

const p = projects.find((x) => x.slug === 'andon')!;
const num = (s: string) => Number(s.replace('%', ''));
---
<Base
  title="Andon: hearing when a machine sounds wrong | Abtin Aryanpour"
  description="A machine-sound anomaly detector that learns only from healthy recordings and turns a green, yellow or red light when a machine starts to sound wrong."
  current="work"
>
  <article class="proj">
    <header class="top">
      <div class="top-sky"><ProjectTrail slug="andon" /></div>
      <div class="top-text wrap">
        <h1 class="display">Andon</h1>
        <p class="lead">A machine-sound anomaly detector that learns only from healthy recordings, and turns a green, yellow or red light when a machine starts to sound wrong.</p>
        <p class="status">Solo project: I designed and built all of it. Started in October 2026 and still in progress. The software stage is done and the hardware stage has started. <a href="https://github.com/abtinmbm/andon">Code on GitHub</a></p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="sec first wrap grid" aria-labelledby="why-h">
      <div class="side">
        <h2 id="why-h" class="visually-hidden">Why it exists</h2>
        <p class="lede">Factories use andon lights to flag a problem on the line. But a fault is rare and expensive to record, so the system has to learn from normal sound alone.</p>
        <p class="lede-more">A small CNN learns to recognise each individual machine by its sound. When it can no longer tell which machine it’s hearing, something has changed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="main">
        <AndonReplay captionId="replay-cap">
          <figcaption id="replay-cap" class="small">The real scores from one fan (machine id_06, development test clips, model seed 0), replayed at 8× speed: about two minutes of normal running, then a fault. The yellow light partway through is a real warning that clears by itself without stopping the line. This machine was picked to show how the light works. Typical rates are below.</figcaption>
        </AndonReplay>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec wrap grid" aria-labelledby="how-h">
      <div class="side"><h2 id="how-h">From a sound to a light</h2></div>
      <ol class="main steps">
        <li><p><b>Listen.</b> Each 10-second clip becomes a log-mel spectrogram: how loud each of 128 frequency bands is, every 32 ms.</p></li>
        <li><p><b>Recognise.</b> One small CNN per machine type learns to tell that type’s machines apart, from normal recordings only.</p></li>
        <li><p><b>Score.</b> If the CNN can’t recognise the machine it’s hearing, the score rises. Each 2-second window gets a score, every second.</p></li>
        <li><p><b>Set limits.</b> Normal audio sets a warning limit and a control limit for each machine, like a factory control chart (mean + 2σ and mean + 3σ of normal scores). They’re the two dashed lines in the replay.</p></li>
        <li><p><b>Light.</b> Two windows in a row over the warning limit turn it yellow. Three in a row over the control limit turn it red, and it stays red until someone resets it.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="sec wrap grid" aria-labelledby="results-h">
      <div class="side">
        <h2 id="results-h">Better than the reference on five of six machine types</h2>
        <p class="note">AUC is the chance a random faulty clip scores as more abnormal than a random normal one. 50% is guessing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="main">
        <AucChart />
        <div class="two-col">
          <p><b class="num">{andon.meanAucSpread}</b> mean AUC (3 seeds) on the held-out DCASE 2020 evaluation set, against {andon.baselineAucSpread} for the reference autoencoder.</p>
          <p>Before changing anything I reproduced the published reference on the development set, to within <b class="num">{andon.reproductionGap}</b> AUC points on all six machine types.</p>
          <p>Played back as a continuous stream of test audio, the light caught <b class="num">{andon.faultsCaught}</b> of 30-second faults, on average {andon.secondsToRed} after they started, with {andon.falseAlarmsPerHour} false alarms per hour.</p>
          <p>It has {andon.weights} weights. As int8 it’s <b class="num">{andon.int8Size}</b>, down from {andon.floatSize}, and still scores {andon.int8Auc} AUC.</p>
          <p>The checkpoints are public, and two commands re-check the held-out CNN AUC results from a fresh clone.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="story">
      <h2 class="visually-hidden">What was hard</h2>
      <section class="sec wrap grid" aria-labelledby="hard-1">
        <div class="side"><h3 id="hard-1">There were no faults to train on</h3></div>
        <div class="main pair">
          <p>Instead of learning what a fault sounds like, the CNN learns to name which machine it hears. Low confidence in the right name becomes the anomaly score. On the evaluation set, valve went from {andon.valveAe} AUC with the reference to {andon.valveCnn}.</p>
          <figure class="ev specs">
            <div class="spec"><Picture src={specNormal} formats={['avif', 'webp']} fallbackFormat="webp" widths={[480, 939]} sizes="(max-width: 899px) 92vw, 30vw" alt="Spectrogram of a normal clip: steady bright bands in the low frequencies." /><span class="small">Normal</span></div>
            <div class="spec"><Picture src={specFault} formats={['avif', 'webp']} fallbackFormat="webp" widths={[480, 939]} sizes="(max-width: 899px) 92vw, 30vw" alt="Spectrogram of a faulty clip from the same fan: the bands look almost the same." /><span class="small">Faulty</span></div>
            <figcaption class="small">Two 10&nbsp;s clips from the replay fan (id_06), as the model hears them: 128 frequency bands, low pitches at the bottom. The fault is hard to see, and training only ever hears the first kind.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="sec wrap grid" aria-labelledby="hard-2">
        <div class="side"><h3 id="hard-2">Overconfidence hurt the score</h3></div>
        <div class="main pair">
          <p>Without mixup, the ToyConveyor classifier hit 100% training accuracy, which likely left little room for a fault to lower its confidence. Blending pairs of training examples (mixup) raised mean development AUC from {andon.devAucNoMixup} to {andon.devAucMixup}.</p>
          <div class="ev">
            <Bars min={50} max={100} ticks={[50, 75, 100]} unit="%" caption="Mean AUC on the development set. 50% is guessing."
              rows={[{ label: 'No mixup', to: num(andon.devAucNoMixup), text: andon.devAucNoMixup }, { label: 'Mixup', to: num(andon.devAucMixup), text: andon.devAucMixup, strong: true }]} />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
      <section class="sec wrap grid" aria-labelledby="hard-3">
        <div class="side"><h3 id="hard-3">PyTorch doesn’t say how it rounds</h3></div>
        <div class="main">
          <p>Its quantized kernels don’t specify their rounding, and a C port has to match them exactly. So I wrote a NumPy fixed-point reference of {andon.quantRefLines} lines that defines every integer step, and exports test vectors the C version must match.</p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="sec wrap grid" aria-labelledby="short-h">
      <div class="side"><h2 id="short-h">Where it still falls short</h2></div>
      <div class="main">
        <p>AUC isn’t real-world performance. {andon.meanAuc} AUC still caught only {andon.faultsCaught} of faults on a continuous stream of test audio, and {andon.valveCaught} for valve. {andon.falseAlarmsPerHour} false alarms per hour is too many for a real line. A much simpler Gaussian mixture beats the CNN on {andon.gmmWins} machine types. And the compute figure for the Pico ({andon.estComputeMs} per 32 ms of audio) is an estimate until it runs on the device.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="sec wrap grid" aria-labelledby="next-h">
      <div class="side"><h2 id="next-h">Next, a Raspberry Pi Pico 2 that cuts power to a real fan the moment it hears a fault</h2></div>
      <div class="main">
        <p>The first firmware exists: the andon LEDs and a debounced button in C, tested on the PC and cross-compiled in CI. The light’s full logic is still in Python, and the model doesn’t run on the device yet. Next come the fan switch, a microphone, a dataset of the real fan, and the model in C.</p>
        <p class="small built">Built with Python, PyTorch, scikit-learn, Streamlit, C and the Pico SDK.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <nav class="onward wrap" aria-label="Next project">
      <a href="/projects/image-stitcher/">Next project: Image Stitcher</a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</Base>

